, capped at
).

remarks.para:
closes that gap with a fail-fast startup guard. It owns a lock file (JSON: the owning process id + a host identifier). On :
no lock file → create it and acquire;
a lock file whose owner process is not alive (the prior instance exited or crashed and did not clean up) → steal it (overwrite) and acquire — so a crash never leaves a stale lock that bricks the instance;
a lock file whose owner process is alive and is a different process → throw () so the host fails to start with an actionable message.

member.summary:
Waits until at least one in-flight delivery task has completed (removing it from
), or the set is empty (returning immediately). Used by the pump to
wait for the drain without spinning. A host shutdown is handled by the caller, which stops
looping (and the in-flight deliveries themselves observe the cancellation on their next await);
this wait therefore has no cancellation of its own — it only completes when a delivery finishes.

Schedules an activity for delivery to the recipient actor's inbox. The inbox IRI is resolved
from the recipient's advertised endpoints.sharedInbox (when its document advertises one)
and otherwise falls back to the ActivityPub convention (recipientIri + "/inbox").

Serializes a polymorphic  to its ActivityStreams JSON (via
) and reads it back through IObjectOrLink, so the journal preserves
the concrete activity type.

Handles inbound content activities delivered to a community's inbox (,
): records the content in each of the community's local members' outboxes so the
content appears in the community's unified feed.

Yields the mention tokens found in  as (full token, handle, domain). A bare
@handle has a null domain; a federated @user@domain has both. Tokens are de-duplicated
(first occurrence wins) so each is resolved at most once.

Resolves the moving actor's key IRI: reads the cached actor document's publicKey.id (via
) when available, falling back to the ActivityPub
convention actorIri#key-1.

Stores OAuth2 authorization codes and Bearer tokens. The host app provides the backing store
(in-memory, database, Redis). Phase 15.2a introduces the server-side OAuth2 token endpoints;
this is the seam that makes the token store pluggable.

